  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Pleasant/dry pattern with comfortably low daytime \nhumidity continues into the last weekend of July. \n\nSurface high pressure extending from the Lower Great Lakes to\nthe New England states will bring another several days of very\npleasant Mid Summer conditions with abundant sunshine today and\na mix of sun and clouds for Saturday and Sunday.\n\nWhile there could be one or two highly isolated showers or \na thunderstorm over the region late this weekend (mainly over\nwestern portions of the state), the airmass remains quite dry. \n\nHigh temperatures today and Saturday will range from the mid to\nupper 70s across the higher terrain, to the low 80s in the\nCentral and Southern Valleys.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Turning more humid with several rounds of showers\nand thunderstorms next week. Perhaps a few strong storms late \nMonday and Tuesday afternoon/evening.\n\nHighly diffluent northwesterly flow accentuated by a few\nshortwaves dropping southeast across the Great Lakes region \nwill lead to an increasing probability for showers and \nthunderstorms. The highest chance for convection will come \nMonday afternoon and evening and again on Tuesday as a fairly \ncompact, but potent mid and upper level short wave digs SE and \namplifies significantly as it reaches our longitude. \n\nThe initial round of SHRA/TSRA will track SE along a slow-moving\nwarm front and could be on the strong to severe side late\nMonday/Mon evening across parts of Central and Western PA \n\nWPC has a broad level 1/4 marginal excessive rain risk for \nD5/Monday July 27 that covers most of CPA. \n\nSfc low tracking from Lake Superior on Monday and deepening by \nabout 6 mb (to around 995 MB) by the time it reaches Lake \nOntario at 18Z Tuesday will help to focus TSRA in PA during the \nday Tuesday along, and ahead of a surface cold front (that will\nbe over NW PA late Tuesday morning with a well-defined lee \ntrough extending down the Susq and Delaware Valleys Tues \nafternoon/early evening).",
